      "url": "https://www.last.fm/music/Porcupine+Tree/Up+the+Downstair",
      "wiki_content": "Up the Downstair is the second studio album by British progressive rock band Porcupine Tree, first released in May 1993. It was originally intended to be a double album set including the song \"Voyage 34\", which was instead released as a single in 1992, and other material that ended up on the Staircase Infinities EP (1994). In 2005, it was partially re-recorded, fully re-mixed, remastered and re-released along with the Staircase Infinities EP as a double album. The re-release contains a new mix by Steven Wilson, along with recorded drums by Gavin Harrison that replace the electronic drums of the original version. Steven Wilson has stated that the title of the album came from a line in the song \"Voyage 34.\" Another re-release on vinyl was pressed on August 14th, 2008 on Kscope records. This is identical to the 2005 release, except it is printed on coloured vinyl. <a href=\"https://www.last.fm/music/Porcupine+Tree/Up+the+Downstair\">Read more on Last.fm</a>. User-contributed text is available under the Creative Commons By-SA License; additional terms may apply.",
      "wiki_summary": "Up the Downstair is the second studio album by British progressive rock band Porcupine Tree, first released in May 1993. It was originally intended to be a double album set including the song \"Voyage 34\", which was instead released as a single in 1992, and other material that ended up on the Staircase Infinities EP (1994). In 2005, it was partially re-recorded, fully re-mixed, remastered and re-released along with the Staircase Infinities EP as a double album. The re-release contains a new mix by Steven Wilson, along with recorded drums by Gavin Harrison that replace the electronic drums of the <a href=\"https://www.last.fm/music/Porcupine+Tree/Up+the+Downstair\">Read more on Last.fm</a>."
    },
    "perplexity": {
      "description": "Up the Downstair is Porcupine Tree’s second studio album and a key early statement in Steven Wilson’s evolving psychedelic-progressive project. Released in 1993 on Delerium Records, it expanded beyond the looser collage approach of the band’s debut and presented a more coherent, album-like vision of space rock, ambient texture, and hypnotic guitar-driven prog. The record’s title and atmosphere lean into altered-consciousness imagery, giving it a hazy, inward-looking character that helped define Porcupine Tree’s early identity.\n\nThe album is often regarded as one of the band’s first fully realized works, with a stronger sense of focus and musicianship than its predecessor. Its blend of dreamy melodies, long-form structures, and moody instrumental passages drew favorable attention from progressive rock listeners, and it has remained an important part of Porcupine Tree’s catalog. Later remixed and reissued versions further underscored its significance, but the original album stands as a formative and widely admired example of the group’s early psychedelic prog style.",
